      "text": "Let $x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ},$ so we want to determine $\\dfrac{\\tan 80^\\circ}{x}$. Observe that \\[\\dfrac{\\tan 80^\\circ}{x}=\\dfrac{\\tan 80^\\circ}{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}\\cos 80^\\circ}{\\sin 80^\\circ}}=\\dfrac{\\sin 80^\\circ}{\\sqrt{3}\\cos 80^\\circ}.\\]Multiply the top and bottom by $\\cos 80^\\circ$ to obtain \\begin{align*}\\dfrac{\\sin 80^\\circ}{\\sqrt{3}\\cos 80^\\circ}&=\\dfrac{\\sin 80^\\circ\\cos 80^\\circ+\\cos 80^\\circ\\sin 80^\\circ}{\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2 80^\\circ} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{2\\sin (80^\\circ+80^\\circ)}{\\sqrt{3}(1-\\sin^2 80^\\circ)} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{2\\sin 160^\\circ}{\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2 80^\\circ} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{2\\sin(180^\\circ-20^\\circ)}{\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2 80^\\circ} \\end{align*}Finally, substituting $40^\\circ$ in for $20^\\circ,$ we see that \\[\\dfrac{\\sin 160^\\circ}{\\sqrt{3}\\cos^3 80^\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\sin 120^\\circ}{\\cos^3 80^\\circ} =\\dfrac{\\cos 60^\\circ}{\\cos^3 80^\\circ} = \\boxed{2}.\\]. The answer is 2",
