      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\), we can use the angle subtraction and addition formulas along with some trigonometric identities. However, a simpler approach is to recognize that the denominator can be combined and simplified.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\nNotice that we can combine these terms by finding a common denominator, which is \\(\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ\\). So, we rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\nWe know from trigonometric identities that \\(\\sin 80^\\circ = \\cos (90^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 80^\\circ = \\sin (90^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\sin 10^\\circ\\). So we can substitute these into our expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos 10^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\sin 10^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\nWe recognize that \\(\\cos 10^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\sin 10^\\circ\\) can be simplified using a well-known trigonometric identity. Specifically, we can rewrite the numerator as a product of cosine and sine using the identity \\(\\cos (x + 60^\\circ) = \\cos x \\cos 60^\\circ - \\sin x \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos x - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin x\\). Here, \\(x = 10^\\circ\\), so the numerator becomes:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\sin 10^\\circ = \\cos (10^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = -\\cos 50^\\circ.\n\\]\nTherefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{-\\cos 50^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\nWe know from trigonometric identities that \\(\\sin 160^\\circ = \\cos 20^\\circ\\), so \\(\\cos 80^\\circ = \\sin 10^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 80^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ\\). Therefore, the denominator \\(\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ = \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ\\). So the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{-\\cos 50^\\circ}{\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ} = \\frac{-2 \\cos 50^\\circ}{\\sin 20^\\circ}.\n\\]\nWe know from trigonometric identities that \\(\\cos 50^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 20^\\circ = 2 \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ\\). However, a simpler way to recognize this is to use the known identity for \\(\\cos (60^\\circ - \\theta) = \\cos 60^\\circ \\cos \\theta + \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin \\theta = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos \\theta + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin \\theta\\). Here, we recognize that the numerator is \\(\\cos 10^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\sin 10^\\circ = \\cos (30^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\sin 30^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ + \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ\\), which simplifies to \\(\\cos 50^\\circ = -\\frac{1}{2}\\). Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sin 20^\\circ} = -\\frac{1}{2 \\sin 20^\\circ}.\n\\]\nHowever, a simpler solution is to recognize that the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\) can be simplified directly using the known values of trigonometric functions for these angles. From trigonometric identities, we know that \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} = \\sec 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ}\\) and \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\). Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sec 80^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} = 2.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]",
